<ResourceDictionary xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">
  <!--
    ================================================================================================
    THE CONTROL-THEME LAYER — the authoring contract. Read this before writing Pills.axaml,
    Segmented.axaml, Tabs.axaml, Fields.axaml or Keycap.axaml.
    ================================================================================================

    Themes/Controls.axaml puts FluentTheme's own templates on our palette by redeclaring the keys
    they read. That is enough for a plain Button or ComboBox and it is not enough for the redesign:
    a layer pill, a tab, a key cap and a token field are shapes Fluent does not draw, and the old
    way of forcing them — a class selector in Styles/ reaching into Fluent's template through
    `/template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter` — was a hack that broke the moment Fluent
    moved a part. This directory replaces the template outright instead.

    THE CONTRACT
    ============

    1. WRITE YOUR OWN TEMPLATE; NEVER REACH INTO A THEME YOU DO NOT OWN. Each ControlTheme sets its
       own `Template` whose root is a `Border x:Name="Root"` that template-binds Background,
       BorderBrush, BorderThickness, CornerRadius and Padding. A state then sets those properties
       ON THE CONTROL — `<Style Selector="^:pointerover"><Setter Property="Background" .../></Style>`
       — and the template binding carries them to the Border. That is what removes the need for the
       PART_ContentPresenter hack: nothing outranks a value set on the control itself.

    2. TARGETING YOUR OWN TEMPLATE PARTS IS CORRECT. `^:focus-visible /template/ Border#Root` inside
       the ControlTheme that declares that Border is idiomatic Avalonia and is what Fluent itself
       does. The ban is on a class selector in Styles/ reaching into somebody else's template.
       Name parts `Root`, `Halo`, `Underline`, ... — never with a `PART_` prefix.

       One consequence of dropping the `PART_ContentPresenter` name: the ContentPresenter is no
       longer registered with the ContentControl, so button content hangs off the presenter rather
       than off the button in the logical tree. Bindings, styles and DataContext are unaffected; a
       `{Binding $parent[SomeAncestor]}` written INSIDE a themed button's content would not be, so
       put those bindings on the button itself.

    3. THE FOCUS RING — 1px accent border + the 3px 28% halo, never an offset outline, because it
       has to read on a 26px key cap without eating its neighbour. Copy this verbatim:

           <Style Selector="^:focus-visible">
             <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AccentBrush}" />
           </Style>
           <Style Selector="^:focus-visible /template/ Border#Root">
             <Setter Property="BoxShadow" Value="{DynamicResource ShadowFocusHalo}" />
           </Style>

       `:focus-visible` is Avalonia 11.3's own pseudo-class and already has the semantics the design
       asks for — a pointer press does not raise it, Tab and the arrow keys do — so no input-source
       tracking is needed. Suppress Fluent's adorner (`FocusAdorner = {x:Null}`) so the two rings do
       not double up. Anything derived from BaseButton below inherits all of this.

    4. FOCUS AND SELECTION COEXIST AND STAY DISTINGUISHABLE. Selection is the filled face and a ring
       drawn in the accent family; focus is the halo outside it. A control that is both shows both.

    5. STATE PRECEDENCE IS EXPLICIT, NOT INHERITED FROM DECLARATION ORDER. Avalonia has no selector
       specificity — the last matching setter wins — so a matrix that relies on file order breaks
       the moment somebody reorders it. Where two states can be true at once, say so in the
       selector. The two that recur here:
         - the focus ring owns the border whenever it is visible, so every pointer-state BorderBrush
           setter carries `:not(:focus-visible)`;
         - selection outranks hover, so a hover setter on a selectable control carries
           `:not(.selected)`.
       BaseButton's styles are applied before a derived theme's (Avalonia walks `BasedOn` first),
       which is why the qualifiers, not the order, are what make this correct.

    6. `ClipToBounds` STAYS FALSE on anything that draws the halo — a BoxShadow is painted outside
       the Border's own bounds and a clip erases it.

    7. COLOURS ARE ALWAYS `{DynamicResource}`; they live in theme dictionaries and a StaticResource
       would snapshot one variant at load. Shapes from Themes/Geometry.axaml are theme-independent
       and may be `{StaticResource}`. A hex literal outside Themes/Tokens.axaml is a bug and fails a
       test.

    8. NOTHING HERE ANIMATES. The motion budget (Themes/Motion.axaml) lists every animation the app
       may have and a button hover is not on it. Writing our own templates also drops the 75ms
       RenderTransform press animation Fluent's Button ships, which is the reason `Button.layerTab`
       and `Button.editorTab` had to clear `Transitions` by hand.

    This file holds what the rest of the directory shares: the hatch tile, and the base button
    theme that carries the template, the geometry, the type and the focus ring.
  -->

  <ResourceDictionary.ThemeDictionaries>

    <!--
      THE HATCH TILE. Two jobs, one meaning: a locked key ("not yours to edit") and an LED that is
      off — "off is hatched, never black", because black is a colour a key can legitimately be lit.

      Themes/Geometry.axaml fixes the shape at HatchAngle 45 degrees and HatchPitch 4; the pitch is
      spelled as the literal 4 below because a RelativeRect and a path geometry are parsed from
      strings and cannot interpolate a resource. Keep the two in step.

      Drawn as filled bands rather than stroked lines so the drawing's natural bounds are EXACTLY
      the 4x4 tile: a pen would widen the bounds by half its thickness, the tile brush would map
      those wider bounds onto SourceRect, and the pattern would drift out of register at every seam.
      Each band is the region where (x + y) falls in [0, 1.5] modulo 4, which is periodic over the
      tile in both axes — so a stripe leaving the right edge re-enters the next tile exactly where
      it left, at any control size.

      The colour is a role, not a value, and it is declared once per variant here because a brush in
      a plain (non-theme) dictionary would resolve its colour against the APPLICATION's variant
      rather than the window's.
    -->
    <ResourceDictionary x:Key="Dark">
      <DrawingBrush x:Key="HatchBrush"
                    TileMode="Tile"
                    Stretch="Fill"
                    SourceRect="0,0,4,4"
                    DestinationRect="0,0,4,4">
        <DrawingBrush.Drawing>
          <GeometryDrawing Brush="{StaticResource SurfaceLineHighBrush}"
                           Geometry="M 0,0 L 1.5,0 L 0,1.5 Z M 4,0 L 4,1.5 L 1.5,4 L 0,4 Z" />
        </DrawingBrush.Drawing>
      </DrawingBrush>
    </ResourceDictionary>

    <ResourceDictionary x:Key="Light">
      <DrawingBrush x:Key="HatchBrush"
                    TileMode="Tile"
                    Stretch="Fill"
                    SourceRect="0,0,4,4"
                    DestinationRect="0,0,4,4">
        <DrawingBrush.Drawing>
          <GeometryDrawing Brush="{StaticResource SurfaceLineHighBrush}"
                           Geometry="M 0,0 L 1.5,0 L 0,1.5 Z M 4,0 L 4,1.5 L 1.5,4 L 0,4 Z" />
        </DrawingBrush.Drawing>
      </DrawingBrush>
    </ResourceDictionary>
  </ResourceDictionary.ThemeDictionaries>

  <!--
    THE BASE BUTTON. Every button-shaped control in the app is a `Button` — the action buttons, the
    layer pills, the section tabs, a lighting zone chip and the key cap all are — so the template,
    the geometry, the type step, the focus ring and the disabled face are declared once here and
    each concrete theme derives from it with `BasedOn="{StaticResource BaseButton}"` and declares
    only its own colour ramp.

    It paints nothing of its own: a transparent face and a transparent 1px border. Transparent
    rather than absent so that (a) the whole face is hit-testable and (b) a theme that shows a
    border only when focused does not reflow when it appears — the thickness is always there, only
    the brush changes.

    Type is the design's control step, 12/500 sans (docs/design/handoff.md, "Typography"); the
    accent primary lifts to 600 as the mockups draw it.
  -->
  <ControlTheme x:Key="BaseButton" TargetType="Button">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="Transparent" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource RadiusControl}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource PaddingButton}" />
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource FontSizeControl}" />
    <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Medium" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand" />

    <!-- Contract 3: Fluent's dotted adorner would sit on top of our ring. -->
    <Setter Property="FocusAdorner" Value="{x:Null}" />

    <!-- Contract 6: the halo is a BoxShadow with spread, drawn outside Root's bounds. -->
    <Setter Property="ClipToBounds" Value="False" />

    <Setter Property="Template">
      <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
        <Border x:Name="Root"
                ClipToBounds="False"
                Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                BorderBrush="{TemplateBinding BorderBrush}"
                BorderThickness="{TemplateBinding BorderThickness}"
                CornerRadius="{TemplateBinding CornerRadius}"
                Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}">
          <ContentPresenter x:Name="Label"
                            Content="{TemplateBinding Content}"
                            ContentTemplate="{TemplateBinding ContentTemplate}"
                            HorizontalContentAlignment="{TemplateBinding HorizontalContentAlignment}"
                            VerticalContentAlignment="{TemplateBinding VerticalContentAlignment}"
                            RecognizesAccessKey="True" />
        </Border>
      </ControlTemplate>
    </Setter>

    <!-- The neutral disabled face, shared by everything that has a face at all: it leaves whatever
         family the button belongs to entirely, because a Save with nothing to save must not look
         like a Save that works. The chromeless themes (ghost, link, row) override it back to
         transparent. A disabled control is not hit-testable, so it can never also be :pointerover
         or :pressed and needs no qualifier. -->
    <Style Selector="^:disabled">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceInsetBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextDisabledBrush}" />
    </Style>

    <!-- Contract 3. Declared here so no concrete theme has to remember it, and qualified nowhere:
         focus outranks every pointer state, which is why those carry `:not(:focus-visible)`. -->
    <Style Selector="^:focus-visible">
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AccentBrush}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="^:focus-visible /template/ Border#Root">
      <Setter Property="BoxShadow" Value="{DynamicResource ShadowFocusHalo}" />
    </Style>
  </ControlTheme>
</ResourceDictionary>
